    "eventMetadata": {
      "context_description": "Recent U.S. economic data have kept trader odds for the September 15-16 FOMC decision nearly even between holding the federal funds rate at 3.50-3.75% and a 25-basis-point hike. Inflation has remained above the 2% target for more than five years, with core measures showing only gradual moderation amid energy-price volatility tied to Middle East developments, while the labor market has posted mixed results including a stronger-than-expected August jobs report. Fed officials, including Governor Waller, have signaled that the upcoming August CPI and PPI prints will heavily influence whether conditions support steady policy or a modest tightening step. Updated dot-plot projections and hawkish remarks from Chair Warsh have reinforced the possibility of a rate increase, yet softer prior employment and retail-sales figures continue to support a hold. The balance leaves room for separation once the inflation data arrive and clarify the pace of disinflation.",
      "context_requires_regen": true,
      "context_updated_at": "2026-09-06T17:16:39.151Z"
